  "description": "Stars sit BELOW approval in the runoff: the most-starred finalist is eliminated by rule 2, and rule 3 then decides between the survivors",
  "spec_clause": "Phase 2 / Tally: 'Resolve in order: 1. Copeland score - most pairwise victories wins. 2. Approval - the higher-approved finalist wins (the approval fraction, not the raw yes count). 3. Stars - more star_votes among attendees wins. Below approval and not above it: a star may separate finalists that both the pairwise vote and standing approval have tied, and never promotes a film past a better-approved one. 4. Rotation fairness. 5. Shortest runtime. 6. Seeded random.'",
  "rationale": "5 attendees, 3 finalists, N_FINALISTS 5 so all three are promoted. m1: 5 rows, 3 yes (u1, u2, u3, all STARRED) and 2 no -> coverage 1.00, yes 3, approval 3/5 = 0.60, star_votes 3 - the most-starred film of the night. m2: 5 rows, 4 yes (u1 STARRED) and 1 no -> approval 4/5 = 0.80, star_votes 1. m3: 5 rows, 4 yes and 1 no -> approval 0.80, star_votes 0. All three clear COVERAGE_FLOOR (1.00) and APPROVAL_FLOOR (0.60, 0.80, 0.80). Round robin: m1 vs m2 = 3 to 2 -> m1; m2 vs m3 = 3 to 2 -> m2; m1 vs m3 = 2 to 3 -> m3. Perfect cycle, Copeland 1/1/1 -> rule 1 separates nothing. Rule 2 approval ranks and narrows: 0.80 (m2) = 0.80 (m3) > 0.60 (m1), so m1 - stars and all - is eliminated by the rung ABOVE the stars, and only {m2, m3} reach rule 3. Rule 3 stars: m2's 1 > m3's 0 -> m2 wins, decided_by 'stars'. Three readings, three different winners: correct -> m2; a chain that puts stars ABOVE approval (the Phase 1 order, wrongly reused) -> m1 on 3 stars; a chain with no star rung at all -> {m2, m3} stay tied through rule 4 (u2 and u3 both never won and both joined 2026-01-01) and rule 5 shortest runtime picks m3 (96 < 130). So the vector pins the star rung's existence AND its position in one round.",
